WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.030
Oh, hey.

9
00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:30.810
Are you ready for a welcome party that no one asked for and no one wants to throw?

10
00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:32.050
Well, I am.

11
00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.910
And I'm also ready to haul some cube.

12
00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.050
Yeah, let's haul cube today.

13
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.390
Hey, it's Season 8, Episode 20.

14
00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.890
This was written by Steve Healy and directed by Ed HelMs.

15
00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:43.070
Here's your summary.

16
00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:52.030
After being instructed by Robert California to throw Nellie a welcome party, the group decides to prank her by making it intentionally awful.

17
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:58.310
Dwight and Jim reluctantly help Nellie move into her new apartment, and they get a glimpse into her heartbreak.

18
00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:04.270
Meanwhile, Andy and Aaron have an important pit stop to make on their road trip back to Scranton.

19
00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.390
And Andy is going to finally break up with Jessica.

20
00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:08.550
With Aaron.

21
00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:09.290
Present.

22
00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:10.930
I know what to say.

23
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:11.330
I have less to say.

24
00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.750
We have texted about it.

25
00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.790
We have feelings about it.

26
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.530
We have strong feelings about it.

27
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.950
Fast fact number one, guest star Brett Gelman as the magician.

28
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.890
I need to just clap for a second.

29
00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.310
I need to clap for a second.

30
00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:26.870
I love him.

31
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.830
He was so fantastic.

32
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:33.410
We're going to talk a lot about it, but oh my gosh, did this guy make us laugh.

33
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:37.570
I did not know Brett Gelman before he guest starred on The Office.

34
00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.990
He came in, and we didn't know what to do with him.

35
00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:42.910
He was so funny.

36
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.390
He destroyed us all.

37
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.010
He did.

38
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.930
You guys out there probably recognize him from the gazillion things he's been in.

39
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.050
He gets a lot of love for Stranger Things.

40
00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.370
I absolutely loved him in Fleabag and Love.

41
00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.970
Everything he did in Fleabag was perfection.

42
00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.090
I do have a crossover shout out.

43
00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:03.950
Oh, yes?

44
00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:04.550
Yes.

45
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:11.650
Not Monk, but fresh off the boat, he played Dusty Nugget, and I played Amy Chestnut.

46
00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.790
So did you guys work together?

47
00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:14.670
We did not.

48
00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:15.030
Oh.

49
00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.690
But we have a crossover moment.

50
00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:17.750
Crossover connection.

51
00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:23.330
Well, before he was on The Office, he had done a lot of shorts for Funny or Die Presents.

52
00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.770
This ran on HBO, very short-lived.

53
00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.790
He had been on Curb Your Enthusiasm.

54
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.230
He had been on Bored to Death.

55
00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:35.350
And it was our writer, Halstead Sullivan, who pitched Brett for the role.

56
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.890
He didn't know him personally, but he was a fan of his work.

57
00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:45.450
And one of the Funny or Die episodes he had recently done was a 16-minute one-man musical

58
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.290
called 1,000 Cats.

59
00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.910
It is really, really funny.

60
00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:50.990
I watched it.

61
00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:52.250
Brett did it.

62
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.870
He dresses as a black cat.

63
00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.110
He has a headband on with little black ears.

64
00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.590
It's very serious.

65
00:02:58.810 --> 00:02:59.490
Very serious.

66
00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.610
Brett did an interview where he said he had been doing it as a 30-minute live show at

67
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:11.210
Upright Citizens Brigade for like eight years, and then it ended up on the Funny or Die HBO show.

68
00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:19.490
The Funny or Die sketch opened with a video of his character talking about his stage show,

69
00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:23.050
1,000 Cats, and why it's important to the world.

70
00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:23.730
Oh, my gosh.

71
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.050
I think we should listen to it.

72
00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:25.330
Okay.

73
00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.230
1,000 Cats is not a play.

74
00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.350
It's not a musical.

75
00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.390
It's not even really a performance.

76
00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:42.990
It is a universal consciousness that is physicalized through me.

77
00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:49.170
Of course, the appropriate venue is not the theater that I will be performing in.

78
00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:56.390
Really, there's only one venue that would do 1,000 Cats justice, and that venue is the Vatican.

79
00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:09.270
I am performing 1,000 Cats, yet I am not performing 1,000 Cats, because 1,000 Cats doesn't even exist while at the same time.

80
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.330
1,000 Cats is the only thing that exists.

81
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.360
So then it cuts to the performance.

82
00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.360
I saw a part of the performance.

83
00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:24.840
It's amazing.

84
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:32.840
It's 1,000 Cats, but they have 9,000 lives and 9,000 stories, and he's going to tell each one.

85
00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:33.360
Each one.

86
00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:34.120
And sing.

87
00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.080
Sing them.

88
00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:35.720
Yes.

89
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.040
It's a musical.

90
00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.120
It's a musical.

91
00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:42.020
Did we mention it's a one-man musical about 1,000 Cats?

92
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.200
So Halstead was familiar, and when they were breaking down the script, he said,

93
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.560
I know who should play the magician.

94
00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.720
Alison Jones put him on tape.

95
00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.480
Everybody watched this tape and loved him, and, you know, Ed Helms was the director of this.

96
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.340
When they gave Ed the tape, he was so excited because he had known Brett for years, you know,

97
00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.120
from their days of both doing Upright Citizens Brigade, and he was like, yes, yes, he's the guy.

98
00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.820
I can't imagine anyone else playing this part, and I'm sad we don't see more of him.

99
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.920
I'm like, why couldn't someone have dated him on the show?

100
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:15.780
Right?

101
00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:16.540
Right?

102
00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.240
Who would it have been?

103
00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:19.540
Angela?

104
00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:21.720
No.

105
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.140
I don't think she would like anything to do with magic.

106
00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.080
She would date the 1,000 Cats guy, though, maybe.

107
00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:27.920
Yes.

108
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.840
Would she be attracted to a man who has a musical about 1,000 Cats?

109
00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:32.740
100%.

110
00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:33.560
I think so.

111
00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:34.420
All right.

112
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.920
Well, let's move on to fast fact number two.

113
00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:41.940
We had a very special cameo on this show that caused a fan mail flurry.

114
00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.840
We got so much mail about it.

115
00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.780
That cameo is the shoe box that Dwight and Jim find in Nellie's apartment.

116
00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:53.340
You know, Benjamin that holds all the photos of Henry.

117
00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:53.600
Yeah.

118
00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:54.880
It's her sad box.

119
00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:55.480
Yes.

120
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.900
Let me explain why this caused a fan mail flurry.

121
00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.540
All of our letters were from folks in the UK.

122
00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:03.780
First off, Becky O.

123
00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.880
from County Durham, England said,

124
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.580
I noticed the shoe box is for the brand Clark's Shoes.

125
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.820
This is pure perfection.

126
00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.100
Clark's is such a well-known British shoe brand.

127
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:20.160
I remember before a new school term, my mom would take me to Clark's and it would be so,

128
00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:20.960
so busy.

129
00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.900
You would have to get a ticket and have to wait till they called your ticket number

130
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.680
for a foot measure and then choose your school shoes.

131
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.160
Any box could have been used for this, but the commitment and attention to detail to use an

132
00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.560
iconic British high street brand was just perfection.

133
00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.040
I am obsessed with this detail.

134
00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.040
Was this a Phil Shea decision?

135
00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:43.480
I cannot express how amazing I think this kind of detail is.

136
00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:44.760
That is fantastic.

137
00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.720
I totally missed that.

138
00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:47.660
I missed it too.

139
00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:48.420
Oh my gosh.

140
00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.120
Thank you for writing in about it.

141
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:50.920
Anna B.

142
00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.840
from Lancashire, United Kingdom said,

143
00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.500
I need to know everything about the amazing shoe box choice in this episode.

144
00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:01.080
This is exactly where someone like Nellie would have bought her work shoes from.

145
00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.440
And Liam C.

146
00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.160
in Glasgow, Scotland said,

147
00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:08.780
I love that Nellie's box of photos, aka Benjamin, was in a Clark's shoe box.

148
00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:13.260
I have fond memories of my mom taking me to get my feet measured at the end of the summer

149
00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.140
before starting a new school year.

150
00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.980
As always, props to the prop department.

151
00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:19.500
That's so great.

152
00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:25.120
Well, everyone, I checked the script and there was no mention of the kind of shoe box.

153
00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:30.040
This was 100% Phil Shea paying attention to detail.

154
00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.500
I love that it resonated with so many people.

155
00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.700
This is just a sample of the letters we got.

156
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.620
And I love that the memories it brought back were people doing their before school shopping,

157
00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.380
foot measure and shoe shopping.

158
00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.620
Yeah, I remember doing that going.

159
00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:48.220
There was one children's shoe store in the mall and you would go and get your foot measured

160
00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.400
and pick out a pair of sneakers or whatever.

161
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.480
This is such a sweet memory.

162
00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.720
And I love that Phil got it.

163
00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:55.560
He nailed it.

164
00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:56.400
Nailed it.

165
00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.560
Well, I thought it might be fun to do a small deep dive on Clark's,

166
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.620
this company that delighted people so much.

167
00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:04.520
Great.

168
00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:05.760
So here we go.

169
00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.980
Clark's is a British international shoe manufacturer and retailer.

170
00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:17.060
It was founded in 1825 by Cyrus Clark in the small village of Sweet Somerset, England.

171
00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:19.780
This is the most adorable small town.

172
00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.060
I looked at pictures.

173
00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:21.280
Did you Google it?

174
00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:21.620
Of course.

175
00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:22.680
Did you look at images of it?

176
00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:23.220
Yes.

177
00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:23.660
Okay.

178
00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:24.300
Of course.

179
00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:25.280
I want to go.

180
00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:26.720
I already know I want to go.

181
00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:27.900
I know I do too.

182
00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.920
This town, it only has about 11,000 people living there,

183
00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.720
but the Clark's World Headquarters is still there.

184
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:34.880
Love that.

185
00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.360
The company has about 1,400 branded stores and franchises around the world.

186
00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.920
You know there's one in our mall, but I wonder if it's the same company.

187
00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:45.160
It has to be, right?

188
00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.680
Now I'm excited to go there even more.

189
00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.100
The company is very well known for their desert boot and their Wallaby.

190
00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:55.480
The Wallaby is what Walter White wore on Breaking Bad.

191
00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:58.680
And of course, children's school shoes.

192
00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.720
I am pretty sure that I wore Clark's on The Office.

193
00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:07.100
I think they do really comfy work shoes for the lady executive.

194
00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.960
You know, it's like a heel, but it's like a chunky heel.

195
00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:11.240
Yeah.

196
00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.420
A chunky low heel.

197
00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:12.780
Yeah.

198
00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:13.860
With good arch support.

199
00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:15.720
I mean, we all know I wore sass.

200
00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:18.980
And then The Working Man, they've got some nice loafers.

201
00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.360
I've been all over the website, clearly.

202
00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.640
But here's what I needed to know.

203
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.340
Is there a Clark's factory that we can tour?

204
00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:31.840
Is it in England, in that cute little town?

205
00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.800
No, that's where the headquarters are.

206
00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.380
No, there is no factory tour.

207
00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:44.280
I did find the Clark's Shoe Museum, but unfortunately, it closed in 2019 after 70 years.

208
00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.120
It just closed.

209
00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.540
I found a blog called Travel with Kevin and Ruth.

210
00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:50.260
Okay.

211
00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:53.120
They're a Canadian couple who are in their early 60s.

212
00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:57.640
They've been married for 39 years, and they blogged all about visiting the Clark's Shoe Museum.

213
00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:58.780
They have great photos.

214
00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:02.320
But unfortunately, that's as close as we're going to get, Ange.

215
00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:06.240
Can I please put Kevin and Ruth's blog link in our stories?

216
00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:07.920
Because I'm sure they would be delighted.

217
00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:09.220
I would love to check it out myself.

218
00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.480
They have a great blog.

219
00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:13.620
They have lots of pictures, and they just travel all around.

220
00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:14.700
Amazing.

221
00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:15.220
Yeah.

222
00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.600
Maybe we should meet up with them somewhere interesting.

223
00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:18.620
Kevin and Ruth.

224
00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:19.620
We can be on their blog.

225
00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:21.180
All right.

226
00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:25.560
Finally, fast fact number three, office ladies' announcements.

227
00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:26.180
Yes.

228
00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.880
These announcements are based on some popular mailbag letters.

229
00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:30.940
Yes, they are.

230
00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:32.060
In our general mailbox.

231
00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:32.560
Mm-hmm.

232
00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:38.660
First off, Peyton from Georgia and many others sent in requests asking if we would please update

233
00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:41.360
the office ladies' Instagram story highlights.

234
00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:42.440
I did it, guys.

235
00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:44.120
I did it.

236
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.160
I sat down one day and updated them all.

237
00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.700
I'm sorry, I got a little behind.

238
00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:49.540
Yes.

239
00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:55.540
We are very excited to announce that all past episodes' stories are now available, so when

240
00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:59.340
you're listening and you hear us say, we're going to put it on the pod, you don't have

241
00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.880
to worry if you missed the day that we posted.

242
00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:03.140
Just go to the highlights.

243
00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:04.180
Go to the highlights.

244
00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.960
Next up, Angela, let's talk mugs.

245
00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:08.720
Okay.

246
00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:13.740
Suzanne in New York wants to know, where did they get Pam's pink mug on the show?

247
00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:17.220
So, Suzanne wanted to know, Jenna, was this mug originally yours?

248
00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.680
If so, where did you get it?

249
00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.340
Is there any brand on the bottom of the mug?

250
00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:23.520
I don't know where they got it, Suzanne.

251
00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:24.100
I looked.

252
00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:25.860
There's no logo anywhere on the mug.

253
00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:27.740
Everyone knows I still have it.

254
00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.460
It broke during season nine and they glued it back together for me.

255
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:31.120
Perfectly.

